        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "An acquired complex hemostatic defect arising from a variety of inciting causes that leads to intravascular activation of coagulation and consumption of clotting factors. It results in widespread formation of microthrombi with clinical manifestations of thrombosis and/or hemorrhage. Non-overt disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is the early, compensated form of DIC that features consumption of coagulation factors and generation of microthrombi without clear clinical signs. Overt (decompensated) DIC refers to the classic phenotype associated with hemorrhage, thrombosis, and organ failure.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "DIC represents a complication of a variety of primary conditions. It begins with a hypercoagulable state that leads to production or embolization of microthrombi in small vessels.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The primary conditions act through increased exposure/production of tissue factor (TF) that activates the extrinsic coagulation pathway.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "TF is normally restricted from intravascular exposure. Increased TF exposure occurs through widespread endothelial injury and/or inflammation.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammation activates endothelial cells, platelets, and monocytes leading to membrane expression of TF. Inflammatory cytokines also induce vesiculation of these membranes, releasing large quantities of microparticles into circulation that are enriched with both TF and phosphatidylserine (PS) and facilitate initiation of coagulation. Some neoplastic cells constitutively produce membrane TF and also release microparticles.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Microparticles provide a suitable membrane surface for amplifying intrinsic and common pathway coagulation, potentially leading to uncontrolled production of thrombin that overwhelms endogenous coagulation inhibitors. Fibrin clots generated by thrombin can cause vascular occlusion and lead to organ dysfunction.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Widespread microthrombus formation consumes coagulation factors and platelets while initiating fibrinolysis. By-products of fibrinolysis (fibrin degradation products [FDPs]) have anticoagulant properties and inhibit platelet function. Hemorrhage at a variety of sites can follow.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Uncontrolled progression leads to widespread tissue hypoxia, multiorgan dysfunction, and death.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Multisystemic syndrome.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
