    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Key differentials—immune-mediated thrombocytopenia, anticoagulant toxicity, coagulation factor deficiency, paraproteinemia.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Highly variable diagnostic pattern includes thrombocytopenia, prolonged clotting times (prothrombin time [PT], activated partial thromboplastin time [APTT]), decreased fibrinogen, decreased antithrombin (AT), and increased products of fibrinolysis (FDPs, D-dimers).",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Suspect DIC any time thrombocytopenia and prolonged clotting tests are seen together.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Patients showing predisposing conditions should have laboratory monitoring every 24–48 hours. A sudden drop in platelet count and a 20–30% prolongation in APTT is suspicious for non-overt DIC. This is a critical stage for intervention to prevent progression to overt DIC.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hepatic insufficiency may mimic DIC. Decreased production of clotting factors is common. Decreased clearance of normal fibrin(ogen)olytic by-products may increase FDP values. Mild idiopathic thrombocyto penia may also be seen. Spontaneous bleeding is uncommon unless DIC is present.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammatory leukogram, often with a stress component.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mild to moderate thrombocytopenia (40–100 × 103/μL); less reliable in cats.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anemia is possible. Red blood cell (RBC) fragmentation is a supportive finding.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Biochemical changes reflect affected organs; acute kidney injury may result in isosthenuria, oligo-anuria, or the identification of casts in urine sediment.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Prolonged clotting tests (PT, APTT); APTT is prolonged first, PT becomes prolonged with transition to overt DIC.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypofibrinogenemia, although inflammatory increase may mask consumption.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Increased FDPs and D-dimers. D-dimers are very sensitive and specific. DIC is unlikely if D-dimers are low/negative. Neither test is specific enough alone to diagnose DIC.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Decreased AT; may be a positive acute phase reactant in cats, masking consumption.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thromboelastography may provide evidence of hypocoagulability or fibrinolysis.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diagnostic procedures should be focused on identifying the inciting cause of inflammation, and may include imaging, tissue biopsy, or surgery as dictated by clinical signs.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Usually related to the primary disease or DIC-affected organs.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Petechiae common.",
              "pdf_page": 495,
              "printed_page": 413,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "GENERAL": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
